<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Afdal Shahanshah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1066">1066</a>–<a href="/years/1121">1121</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Vizier al-Afdal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Afdal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahanshah">Shahanshah</a> was a powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> in the late eleventh and early twelfth centuries. In the succession crisis of 1094 he bypassed the designated heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizar</a> in favour of al-Musta'li. This split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> community permanently into rival branches and damaged the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawa">da'wa</a>'s universal authority.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Afdal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahanshah">Shahanshah</a> was born Abu al-Qasim Shahanshah bin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-jamali">Badr al-Jamali</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, the son of Badr al-Jamali, an Armenian mamluk who became Muslim. Badr was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> from 1074 until his death in 1094, when al-Afdal succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mustansir Billah died soon after Badr, and al-Afdal appointed as caliph al-Musta'li, a child, in place of his much older brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizar-ibn-al-mustansir">Nizar ibn al-Mustansir</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizar</a> revolted and was defeated in 1095, which led to tension between al-Afdal and Nizar's supporters, chiefly Hassan-i Sabbah and his Nizari Isma'ili group, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>. In 1097 al-Afdal captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, and in 1098 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> for Byzantine mercenaries and hoped for an alliance against the Seljuks, but they took Jerusalem on 15 July 1099, and on 12 August 1099 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> surprised and completely defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ascalon">Battle of Ascalon</a>.</p><p>When al-Musta'li died in December 1101 al-Afdal raised the five-year-old al-Amir bi-Ahkam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> to the throne and married him to his own daughter. Under him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> were confined to their palace and lost their ceremonial functions, and he built his own palace, the Dar al-Mulk, outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1106–1108. He was defeated at the Third Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> in 1105 and lost Tripoli in 1109. He also introduced an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a>' tax reform that lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>An assassin tried to kill al-Afdal in 1115, and another attack failed in 1118. He was assassinated in December 1121 during a procession on the last day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a>. The deed was commonly attributed to the Nizari Assassins, and modern scholars commonly accept their responsibility, but the Syrian chronicler Ibn al-Qalanisi blamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> and al-Afdal's chief of staff, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> al-Bata'ihi, who succeeded him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. Al-Amir confiscated his enormous wealth. Little survives of his buildings: the only surviving work is the mihrab of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-of-ibn-tulun">Mosque of Ibn Tulun</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Afdal Shahanshah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the succession crisis of 1094, <b>Vizier al-Afdal</b> bypassed the designated heir Nizar in favour of al-Musta'li, which permanently split the Ismaili community and destroyed the da'wa's universal authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Acre, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.93,35.08&amp;year=1101" aria-label="Acre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5449 968 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of al-Afdal Shahanshah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5899 1238h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5899 1238h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.93,35.08&amp;year=1101">What was here in 1101? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
